1
00:00:01,450 --> 00:00:05,924
In 1930, the economist John Maynard Keynes made a prediction:

2
00:00:06,024 --> 00:00:12,140
a century on, machines would do most of the work, and people would need to work just fifteen hours a week.

3
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:15,693
The century is nearly up, and machines have indeed grown ever more capable,

4
00:00:15,793 --> 00:00:18,241
yet we are no more at ease.

5
00:00:18,281 --> 00:00:23,749
When machines can produce everything, who should the wealth belong to?

6
00:00:28,999 --> 00:00:34,528
Looking back over seventy thousand years, every leap humankind made came with a great change in the way we work.

7
00:00:34,628 --> 00:00:39,754
The Cognitive Revolution taught Sapiens to organise hundreds, even thousands, through shared stories.

8
00:00:39,854 --> 00:00:42,515
The Agricultural Revolution brought surplus grain,

9
00:00:42,615 --> 00:00:46,930
and with it granaries, private property, classes and the state.

10
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:49,781
The Industrial Revolution replaced muscle with steam,

11
00:00:49,881 --> 00:00:53,969
and turned millions of peasants into wage workers in factories.

12
00:00:54,009 --> 00:01:02,297
The hand-mill gives you society with the feudal lord; the steam-mill, society with the industrial capitalist.

13
00:01:02,337 --> 00:01:06,610
When the forces of production change, the relations of production must sooner or later follow.

14
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:11,498
Harari says humans are bound together by the stories they share;

15
00:01:11,598 --> 00:01:17,321
historical materialism asks further: behind the stories, who does the work, and who owns the wealth?

16
00:01:19,821 --> 00:01:23,583
Today, a new "steam engine" is roaring to life.

17
00:01:23,683 --> 00:01:29,000
Like electricity, AI is a general-purpose technology; it will seep into every industry.

18
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:33,841
This time, the first to be hit is not the factory, but the office tower.

19
00:01:34,141 --> 00:01:39,264
Throughout history, technology has created new jobs even as it destroyed old ones.

20
00:01:39,364 --> 00:01:47,111
One forecast says that by 2030 the world will gain 170 million jobs and lose 92 million.

21
00:01:47,311 --> 00:01:50,454
And even the last refuge, manual work,

22
00:01:50,554 --> 00:01:56,259
is narrowing, as embodied AI moves into warehouses, building sites and kitchens.

23
00:01:58,759 --> 00:02:04,024
Homo Deus fears the twenty-first century may create a vast "useless class".

24
00:02:04,124 --> 00:02:06,964
But the real danger is not that people become useless;

25
00:02:07,064 --> 00:02:11,192
it is that wealth piles up like mountains, while most people lose their income.

26
00:02:11,292 --> 00:02:15,680
The question shifts from "Will there be work?" to "How will the wealth be shared?"

27
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,493
This is the contradiction historical materialism reveals:

28
00:02:18,793 --> 00:02:23,181
the forces of production race ahead, while the relations of production stand still.

29
00:02:23,681 --> 00:02:28,333
Some have tried to slam on the brakes: two hundred years ago, the Luddites smashed the looms;

30
00:02:28,433 --> 00:02:31,851
the sea bans of the Ming and Qing dynasties once locked the ports.

31
00:02:31,951 --> 00:02:34,678
But the flood of technology has never been held back.

32
00:02:34,778 --> 00:02:39,704
What we can decide is what kind of channels to build, and where the water will flow.

33
00:02:42,204 --> 00:02:45,639
Before us lie three tomorrows.

34
00:02:45,739 --> 00:02:49,981
In the first, computing power and data are concentrated in a few hands.

35
00:02:50,081 --> 00:02:55,126
21 Lessons warned long ago: whoever owns the data owns the future.

36
00:02:55,226 --> 00:03:01,062
A few become lords of the cloud; everyone else receives charity, not rights.

37
00:03:01,462 --> 00:03:07,378
In the second, a little welfare, a little tax, and the old order is patched up and kept running.

38
00:03:07,778 --> 00:03:12,465
In the third, every one of us becomes a co-owner of the machines.

39
00:03:12,565 --> 00:03:20,332
The wealth AI creates flows back to everyone, through public funds, citizens' dividends and shorter working hours.

40
00:03:20,432 --> 00:03:26,169
Work is no longer merely a means of survival; people are free to create, to learn, and to care for one another.

41
00:03:26,209 --> 00:03:34,234
The free development of each is the condition for the free development of all.

42
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:38,368
But such a tomorrow will not arrive on its own.

43
00:03:38,468 --> 00:03:43,104
The eight-hour day was won by workers taking to the streets, again and again;

44
00:03:43,144 --> 00:03:47,073
Technology decides what is possible; institutions decide what happens.

45
00:03:47,113 --> 00:03:50,858
And institutions are chosen by people, and won by people.

46
00:03:51,058 --> 00:03:54,649
As Marx wrote, people make their own history.

47
00:03:55,099 --> 00:03:57,768
So, whose tomorrow is it?

48
00:03:57,868 --> 00:04:02,246
It belongs to everyone who strives for it, together.

